8. Enos-123 — traffic accident detector
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Author   : Enos-123 (Hugging Face)
Source   : https://huggingface.co/Enos-123/traffic-accident-detection-yolo11x
Licence  : MIT
Used for : Traffic incident detection, retained explicitly as a generality
           experiment for the temporal validator rather than as a disaster
           hazard in its own right.
Condition: A YOLO11x derivative, so it is converted to ONNX in a separate
           build environment containing ultralytics; the runtime environment
           contains neither ultralytics nor torch. Two published checkpoints
           exist — we ship epoch61 rather than the epoch14 that the upstream
           infer.py points at, because epoch61 was better behaved on the
           author's own test images. The author states it is not validated for
           critical autonomous driving use.

================================================================================
PLANNED COMPONENTS — attribution to be completed on integration
================================================================================

None outstanding. All five detectors are integrated and credited above:
fire (1-3), building access (5), flood (6), people in water (7) and traffic (8).

Two entries were removed from this section rather than completed. Flood
segmentation was listed here as an MMSegmentation fine-tune; the flood detector
was instead built on torchvision DeepLabV3, because torchvision is BSD-3-Clause
and keeps the detector clear of the AGPL perimeter. Its dataset credit is now
section 6. Pool-drowning detection was dropped entirely by the scope revision of
1 September 2026 — the Figshare and Water Behavior datasets were never used, and
open-water people detection (section 7) replaced that hazard because it is what
the 2024 Valencia DANA actually involved.

================================================================================
PRIOR ART
================================================================================

VIGÍA is not the first camera-based hazard detection system, and does not claim
to be. Work that precedes and in several respects exceeds it:

  Pano AI                  wildfire detection at scale, commercial
  ALERTCalifornia          UC San Diego / CAL FIRE, 1,060+ cameras
  DigitalPath              wildfire AI, with ALERTCalifornia
  PyroNear                 open-source wildfire detection, deployed in
                           France, Spain and Chile — and the direct source of
                           our fire detector
  Lynxight, AngelEye,      pool drowning detection, commercially mature
    Coral Detection          (ASTM F3698-24, ISO 20380:2017)
  Rekor, Iteris,           traffic incident detection, deployed with US DOTs
    Miovision, NoTraffic
  xView2 / DIUx            building damage assessment from satellite imagery
  RipVIS authors           rip current segmentation benchmark, CVPR 2025

